          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does \"code first\" mean, and what actually goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round?",
          "answer": "**What \"code first\" means**\n\nIn ICD-10-CM, \"code first\" is an instruction about order. When a category carries it, the underlying condition is reported before its manifestation. The conventions document says \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" work as a pair that fixes sequencing. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**What goes wrong if the order is reversed**\n\nThe retrieved material does not say what happens when the sequence is reversed. It does not describe the consequences of a wrong order, and I can't fill that gap from general knowledge. You would need the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or a trained medical coder to answer that part.\n\n**What the sources do say**\n\n- \"Code also\" means two codes are needed but does not set which comes first. \"Code first\" does set the order.\n- Some families use combination codes that name a condition and its complication in one code. Where one exists, it is used instead of separate codes. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions*.)\n- Where the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says they are unrelated. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- Some codes, such as chronic kidney disease staging, are paired with a second code that gives the detail. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*.)\n\nIf you are working on real records, check the instruction in the official tabular list for the specific category and ask a certified coder about sequencing errors.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
